<Guide lang="en" slug="asbestos-uralita-roof-spain" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="renovatie" ctaTitle="Uralita on your roof or water tank?" ctaText="We have it assessed and arrange a licensed contractor, the work plan and a new roof. You get one clear price up front.">
  <h2 id="short">The short answer</h2>
  <p>Many Spanish homes built before 2002 contain asbestos, mostly in corrugated roofs, water tanks and pipes made of <em>uralita</em> (asbestos cement). As long as it is intact and left alone, the risk is low. But you may not demolish it yourself: only a licensed company may remove it, with an approved work plan. Because of those fixed steps, even a small job is never really cheap. If the roof has to go anyway, combine it with insulation or solar panels.</p>

  <h2 id="where">Where uralita is found in a Spanish home</h2>
  <p><em>Uralita</em> is the brand name of Spain’s largest manufacturer, but in Spain it has become the everyday word for asbestos cement (<em>fibrocemento</em>). It was used everywhere until the 1990s and banned in 2002. In the Axarquía you mostly find it in:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrugated roofs</strong> on garages, carports, sheds, canopies and extensions, and on older fincas sometimes on the whole house.</li>
    <li><strong>Rooftop water tanks</strong> (<em>depósitos</em>): the grey round or square tanks still standing on the roof terrace of many village houses and fincas.</li>
    <li><strong>Waste pipes and downpipes</strong>, including those built into walls or buried.</li>
    <li><strong>Flues and chimney pipes</strong>, planters and garden walls.</li>
    <li>Less often: <strong>floor tiles</strong> (1970s vinyl) and the adhesive beneath them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="recognise">Recognising asbestos</h2>
  <p>You cannot be certain by eye, but these are strong indications:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The house or extension was built <strong>before 2002</strong>.</li>
    <li>Grey, fibrous sheets or pipes, often with a fine honeycomb pattern on the underside.</li>
    <li>A stamp reading “Uralita”, “Rocalla” or a product code on the sheet.</li>
    <li>The material sounds hard and hollow and is heavier than plastic.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Certainty comes from a <strong>laboratory test</strong> on a small sample, taken safely by a specialist. It is a small cost compared with the rest of the job.</p>

  <h2 id="danger">When is it dangerous?</h2>
  <p>Asbestos is dangerous when fibres are released and you breathe them in. That does not happen by itself. The risk rises when:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the sheets are <strong>broken, cracked or badly weathered</strong>: after forty years of sun the surface becomes brittle and powdery;</li>
    <li>you start <strong>drilling, cutting, sanding or breaking</strong>;</li>
    <li>someone <strong>cleans the roof with a pressure washer</strong> (never do this);</li>
    <li>an old water tank leaks and is broken open.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An intact, undisturbed carport roof is therefore no reason to panic. It is still wise not to put it off for too long: the material gets worse every year, and so does removing it.</p>

  <h2 id="rules">The rules in Spain and Andalusia</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Banned since 2002.</strong> Asbestos may not be sold or installed since June 2002. What is still in place may stay until the end of its life.</li>
    <li><strong>Licensed companies only.</strong> Removal may only be done by a company on the <em>Registro de Empresas con Riesgo por Amianto</em> (<strong>RERA</strong>), under Royal Decree 396/2006.</li>
    <li><strong>An approved work plan.</strong> For every job the contractor submits a <em>plan de trabajo</em> to the Junta de Andalucía’s labour authority. Work may only start once it is approved.</li>
    <li><strong>Disposal at a licensed landfill.</strong> You receive proof that the waste was handled correctly. Keep it; it helps when you sell.</li>
    <li><strong>Town halls are mapping asbestos.</strong> The waste law <em>Ley 7/2022</em> requires town halls to keep a register of asbestos and a removal schedule. Public buildings come first. There is no fixed deadline for private homes yet, but one may follow.</li>
    <li><strong>Permission for the new roof.</strong> Replacing a roof usually needs a <em>licencia de obra menor</em> or a notification (<em>declaración responsable</em>) to the town hall. Homes with AFO status face extra limits; see our guide to <a href="/en/guides/afo-legalisation-andalusia/">AFO and legalisation</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Grants.</strong> EU recovery funds paid for renovation schemes in Andalusia that rewarded asbestos removal. Those are winding down. Ask the town hall whether anything is still open before you commission the work; applying afterwards is rarely possible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="options">Your options at a glance</h2>
  <table class="prices">
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it involves</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Leave it and keep an eye on it</td><td>Only for an intact, undisturbed roof. Do not walk on it, drill it or pressure-wash it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Encapsulate with a coating</td><td>Special paint that binds the fibres. The asbestos stays; the problem is postponed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over-roof with new sheets</td><td>A new roof over the old one. The asbestos stays; not always permitted.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Remove and fit sandwich panels</td><td>Licensed removal, new roof of insulated panels. Common for carports, garages and sheds.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Remove and fit a tiled roof</td><td>For the house itself: new roof with insulation, waterproofing and tiles.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replace an uralita water tank</td><td>Old tank taken away, new plastic tank fitted.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replace an asbestos-cement waste pipe</td><td>Often done with a bathroom or kitchen renovation.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>If it is the roof of the house, also read <a href="/en/costs/roof-and-roof-terrace/">roof and roof terrace</a> and <a href="/en/guides/insulating-your-house-in-spain/">insulating your house</a>: when the roof comes off anyway, that is the best moment to insulate properly.</p>

  <p><strong>Want the uralita taken away?</strong> We arrange it all for you, from the licensed contractor and work plan to the new roof. <a href="/en/request/?dienst=renovatie">Tell us what is on your roof</a> and we will work out the best approach together.</p>

  <h2 id="removal">How removal works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Inspection and quote.</strong> The contractor looks at the roof, the access and what needs to follow.</li>
    <li><strong>Work plan and permission.</strong> The work plan goes to the Junta, and where needed the notification to the town hall.</li>
    <li><strong>Cordoning off.</strong> The area is sealed off and the sheets are wetted so no dust is released.</li>
    <li><strong>Removing sheets whole.</strong> The sheets are unscrewed, not broken, and packed in special bags.</li>
    <li><strong>Disposal and paperwork.</strong> The waste goes to a licensed landfill and you receive the documents.</li>
    <li><strong>New roof.</strong> Usually straight afterwards: insulated sandwich panels or a tiled roof.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="buying">Asbestos when buying or selling</h2>
  <p>If you are buying an older finca or village house, assume there is uralita somewhere, even if only an old water tank. Have it included in the <a href="/en/property-survey/">property survey</a> and use it in the negotiation. If you are selling, a cleaned-up roof with proof of disposal makes a better impression than a weathered corrugated roof. See also <a href="/en/guides/selling-a-house-in-spain/repairs-before-selling/">repairs before selling</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Always ask for the contractor’s <strong>RERA number</strong> and a copy of the approved work plan.</li>
    <li>Combine the work: asbestos off, insulation on and solar panels at the same time. You pay for scaffolding and call-out only once.</li>
    <li>Do not tidy up loose pieces yourself; have the contractor wet them and take them away.</li>
    <li>Ask for a quote that lists removal, disposal and the new roof separately, so you can compare.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">This guide gives general information based on the rules in Spain and Andalusia in November 2026. Rules may change. It is not legal advice.</p>
</Guide>
